import{_ as t,o as n,c as o,ae as i}from"./chunks/framework.YL6nI5ZP.js";const m=JSON.parse(`{"title":"HydroCouple's Modifications to the US EPA SWMM Engine: the OpenSWMM engine and its extensions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/engine-modifications/01-introduction-and-rewrite.md","filePath":"explanation/engine-modifications/01-introduction-and-rewrite.md"}`),a={name:"explanation/engine-modifications/01-introduction-and-rewrite.md"};function s(r,e,c,d,l,h){return n(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="hydrocouple-s-modifications-to-the-us-epa-swmm-engine-the-openswmm-engine-and-its-extensions" tabindex="-1">HydroCouple&#39;s Modifications to the US EPA SWMM Engine: the OpenSWMM engine and its extensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#hydrocouple-s-modifications-to-the-us-epa-swmm-engine-the-openswmm-engine-and-its-extensions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;HydroCouple&#39;s Modifications to the US EPA SWMM Engine: the OpenSWMM engine and its extensions&quot;">​</a></h1><p><em>Technical Reference Document — August 2026</em></p><p><em>Original source (LaTeX): <a href="./../../sources/modificaciones_openswmm.tex">docs/sources/modificaciones_openswmm.tex</a></em></p><blockquote><p>This document describes, at the level of equations and design, the work that the <strong>HydroCouple</strong> group has carried out on the <strong>SWMM</strong> hydraulic simulation engine of the <strong>U.S. Environmental Protection Agency</strong> (US EPA). The <strong>OpenSWMM</strong> engine is not a point modification of the official SWMM 5.x C code: it is a modern rewrite in C++ that pursues two simultaneous goals: (i) <em>bit-for-bit parity</em> with the EPA engine for standard functionality, and (ii) a layer of <em>numerical and platform extensions</em> that the official engine does not have. This document focuses on that second layer: the semi-implicit node continuity formulation, Anderson acceleration of the Picard loop, the dynamic Preissmann slot, virtual junctions, the explicit 1D finite volume solver, the 2D module with 1D–2D coupling, the physical recovery of RDII, bit-exact OpenMP parallelism, and the default behavior changes.</p></blockquote><h2 id="introduction" tabindex="-1">Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Introduction&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="context-openswmm-and-its-relationship-with-us-epa-swmm" tabindex="-1">Context: OpenSWMM and its relationship with US EPA SWMM <a class="header-anchor" href="#context-openswmm-and-its-relationship-with-us-epa-swmm" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context: OpenSWMM and its relationship with US EPA SWMM&quot;">​</a></h3><p>SWMM (<em>Storm Water Management Model</em>) is the US EPA&#39;s model for stormwater and wastewater management. Its 1D hydraulic engine (SWMM 5.x) solves the Saint-Venant equations over a network of nodes and links with three routing formulations (steady flow, kinematic wave and dynamic wave), and has evolved slowly since the 1970s.</p><p>The <strong>OpenSWMM</strong> project (engine <code>openswmm.engine</code>), led by HydroCouple, reimplements that engine from scratch in modern C++ with two design principles:</p><ol><li><strong>Bit-for-bit parity</strong> with the EPA engine for standard functionality: every arithmetic term of the classical solvers reproduces exactly the order of operations and the values of the legacy code (for example, the exponent <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.33333</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.33333</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height:0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">1.33333</span></span></span></span> of Manning friction, the truncated conversion factors, the grouping of floating-point operations). This is validated with a battery of regression tests against the original engine and with <em>manufactured solutions</em>.</li><li><strong>A layer of extensions</strong>: new numerical algorithms and options that official SWMM does not have, aimed at improving the stability, convergence and physical fidelity of transients, together with modern computing architectures (parallelism, GPU, WebAssembly).</li></ol><p>This document deals exclusively with the second layer. The following sections describe each extension with its equations, its motivation and its input syntax. All code references point to the <code>src/engine/</code> directory of the engine (<code>hydraulics/</code>, <code>hydraulics/fv/</code>, <code>2d/</code> and <code>hydrology/</code>).</p><h3 id="the-architectural-rewrite-as-a-foundation" tabindex="-1">The architectural rewrite as a foundation <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-architectural-rewrite-as-a-foundation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The architectural rewrite as a foundation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Although it is not a functional &quot;extension&quot;, the rewrite is the enabler of everything else. The key architectural decisions are:</p><ul><li><strong>Column-oriented storage (SoA)</strong>: node and link data are stored as parallel arrays (<code>NodeData.hpp</code>, <code>LinkData.hpp</code>), which enables contiguous cache access and SIMD vectorization in the inner loops.</li><li><strong>Batched geometry</strong>: the <code>XSectGroups</code> class (<code>XSectBatch.cpp</code>) groups conduits by geometric shape and computes areas, hydraulic radii and water-surface widths of all conduits in vectorizable passes, without shape dispatch inside the inner loop.</li><li><strong>Momentum kernels by category</strong>: each conduit is classified once per iteration into a category (dry, Manning at free surface, closed at full section, Hazen–Williams or Darcy–Weisbach force main) and dispatched to a branchless kernel.</li><li><strong>Bit-exact OpenMP parallelism</strong>: the entire Picard loop runs inside a single <code>omp parallel</code> region (persistent team), with a <code>THREADS n</code> in <code>[OPTIONS]</code>. Bit-for-bit exactness at any number of threads is guaranteed by design: each parallel loop writes disjoint elements (a single producer per element), and the only combine between threads is an order-free integer counter of non-converged nodes. The sweep of flows toward the nodes is done via a CSR node→conduit adjacency that reproduces exactly the floating-point accumulation order of the serial sweep (<code>buildConduitNodeCSR</code>).</li></ul>',13)])])}const u=t(a,[["render",s]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
